From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cristian Vat <cristian.vat@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 15:16:56 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Look for ffmpeg next to the executable before the PATH

On Linux ffmpeg is a package dependency and is found on the PATH. The
Windows zip has no package manager to lean on, so it ships ffmpeg and
ffprobe beside omacut.exe. Look there first, so the bundled build is the
one used even when some other ffmpeg is on the PATH, and fall back to
the PATH for a developer build that bundles nothing.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/ffmpeg.cpp | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/ffmpeg.cpp b/src/ffmpeg.cpp
index fbea5b4f301731e4b10932e4974bb4108c705905..e2911364015c6abd18bdf44c2c4a9c9cf6042a0e 100644
--- a/src/ffmpeg.cpp
+++ b/src/ffmpeg.cpp
@@ -1,5 +1,6 @@
 #include "ffmpeg.h"
 
+#include <QCoreApplication>
 #include <QJsonArray>
 #include <QJsonDocument>
 #include <QJsonObject>
@@ -17,6 +18,14 @@ constexpr int kThumbPollMs = 50;
 }
 
 QString toolPath(const QString &tool) {
+    // The Windows build ships ffmpeg alongside the executable; prefer that over
+    // whatever the PATH happens to offer, so the pair always matches. Falls back
+    // to the PATH, which is how a developer build finds its ffmpeg.
+    const QString bundled =
+        QStandardPaths::findExecutable(tool, {QCoreApplication::applicationDirPath()});
+    if (!bundled.isEmpty())
+        return bundled;
+
     return QStandardPaths::findExecutable(tool);
 }
 
